  "findings": "Compared the slowest 10% Application objects with the slowest 10% Offer objects. The slowest Application threshold is 3023425.0 seconds with 3151 objects out of 31509, while the slowest Offer threshold is 2672931.8 seconds with 4300 objects out of 42995. Slow Applications have a median flow time of 3656215.0 seconds versus 1645576.0 seconds for median Applications, a flow time ratio of 2.38. Slow Offers have a median flow time of 3283889.0 seconds versus 1372916.0 seconds for median Offers, a flow time ratio of 2.58. Structurally, slow Applications do not have more events than median Applications: mean events per object is 8.31 versus 8.62, event count ratio 0.96. Slow Offers also have fewer events than median Offers: 4.42 versus 4.78, event count ratio 0.92. The main disproportionate slow-Application activity is A_Cancelled, with disproportion factor 8.31. For slow Offers, O_Cancelled is strongly disproportionate with factor 2.65, and O_Sent (online only) is also overrepresented with factor 1.42. Application-to-Offer object structure shows that Applications have on average 1.36 related Offers, median 1.0, min 1, max 10, and the number of Offers per Application is positively correlated with Application flow time: Pearson r=0.2202 and Spearman rho=0.1782, both p=0.0. Application-to-Workflow count correlation is undefined because every Application has exactly one Workflow, i.e. zero variance. Activity frequency profiles show Application events are dominated by A_Validating, A_Create Application, A_Concept, A_Accepted, and A_Complete, while Offer events are dominated by O_Create Offer, O_Created, and O_Sent (mail and online). Interaction pattern analysis for selected slow-relevant activities shows simple one-to-one event interactions: Application activities co-occur only with Application and Case_R, while Offer activities co-occur only with Offer and Case_R, always with mean and median 1 object of each type per event.",
